---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import '../styles/prose.css';
import avatar from '../assets/avatar.png';
import { site } from '../data/site';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title={`About — ${site.name}`}
  description="Jason Yang is a Rust engineer based in the UK, leading protocol development at Solstice Labs and building Solana programs and the backend services that keep them running."
>
  <main id="main-content" class="page container about-page" tabindex="-1">
    <section class="intro" aria-labelledby="about-heading">
      <div class="avatar-ring">
        <Image
          class="avatar intro-avatar"
          src={avatar}
          alt={`Portrait of ${site.name}`}
          width={96}
          height={96}
          densities={[1, 2]}
          loading="eager"
        />
      </div>
      <div class="intro-copy">
        <div class="status-pill"><span class="status-dot"></span>{site.status}</div>
        <h1 id="about-heading" class="display">Hi, I’m Jason.</h1>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="article-prose about-prose">
      <p>
        I’m a Rust engineer based in the UK, currently leading protocol development at Solstice
        Labs. I design and build Solana programs and the backend services that keep them running.
      </p>
      <p>
        Before moving into engineering full-time, I spent more than a decade in financial products
        and business leadership at HSBC and JPMorgan. Much of that work involved helping new
        businesses and products take shape. Engineering has expanded how much of that process I can
        own—from working out how a financial product should behave to building and operating the
        software myself.
      </p>

      <h2 id="what-i-build">What I build</h2>
      <p>
        At Solstice, I’ve built a stablecoin, a yield vault and a reusable tranching platform,
        together with the Rust services that support them.
      </p>
      <p>
        The tranching platform is a good example of the work I enjoy. I took an initial financial
        concept, worked through its accounting and risk mechanics, and carried it through protocol
        design, implementation, testing, external audits and launch. I also built the price-feed and
        automation infrastructure needed to operate it and support additional underlying assets.
      </p>
      <p>
        For me, accounting, risk and operational behaviour are part of the engineering, not separate
        concerns. I make the rules and assumptions explicit, structure the code so they can be
        tested, and stay involved once the system is running.
      </p>

      <h2 id="background">Background</h2>
      <p>
        At HSBC, I helped build the China credit-card business from zero to one million cards, later
        leading business planning and proposition development. That meant working across the
        economics of the lending business and the products offered to customers. I subsequently
        moved into the global lending team, working on international credit decisioning and
        fraud-risk initiatives.
      </p>
      <p>
        At JPMorgan, I joined the early Chase UK lending team and owned account-management product
        design through its credit-card launch. My work became increasingly focused on defining how a
        financial system should behave across different scenarios—the calculations, state changes
        and exceptions that the software needed to handle.
      </p>
      <p>I joined Solstice in April 2025, making hands-on engineering my full-time work.</p>

      <h2 id="this-site">This site</h2>
      <p>
        I use small Rust experiments to work through ideas and write up what I learn. Most posts are
        about Rust internals, systems programming and the mechanics behind financial protocols.
      </p>
    </div>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
